In the PDSF basis construction, the hidden state $h$ is decomposed into four strictly orthogonal components ($h_P \perp h_D \perp h_S \perp h_F$):
1. **$P$ (Predictive / $h_P$)**: The prediction direction anchor set by the normalized unembedding vector of the model's argmax predicted token;
2. **$D$ (Discriminative / $h_D$)**: Top-$k$ PCA of the $P$-residuals $H^\perp$ across prompts ($h_D = B_D(B_D^T h^\perp)$);
3. **$S$ (Situational / $h_S$)**: Top-$k$ PCA of the double residual after removing both $P$ and $D$ ($h_S = B_S(B_S^T h^{\perp\perp})$);
4. **$F$ (Framework / $h_F$)**: The high-dimensional residual spanning thousands of dimensions ($h_F = h^{\perp\perp} - h_S$).

The adaptive ranks are defined using the participation ratio (PR):
- For $D$: $k_D = \text{round}(PR)$, clamped to the interval $[2, 12]$.
- For $S$: $k_S = \text{round}(PR)$ (same PR procedure), clamped to the interval $[4, 16]$.

In the PDSF basis construction, the hidden state $h \in \mathbb{R}^d$ at every layer is decomposed into four strictly orthogonal components ($h_P \perp h_D \perp h_S \perp h_F$):

$$h = h_P + h_D + h_S + h_F$$

where:
1. **$h_P$ (Predictive / $P$)**: Rank-1 projection along the normalized unembedding vector of the model's argmax predicted token;
2. **$h_D$ (Discriminative / $D$)**: Top-$k$ PCA of the $P$-residuals $H^\perp$ across prompts ($h_D = B_D(B_D^T h^\perp)$);
3. **$h_S$ (Situational / $S$)**: Top-$k$ PCA of the double residual after removing both $P$ and $D$ ($h_S = B_S(B_S^T h^{\perp\perp})$);
4. **$h_F$ (Framework / $F$)**: The remaining high-dimensional orthogonal complement spanning thousands of dimensions ($h_F = h^{\perp\perp} - h_S$).

The adaptive ranks are defined using the participation ratio (PR):
- For $D$: $k_D = \text{round}(PR)$, clamped to $[2, 12]$;
- For $S$: $k_S = \text{round}(PR)$, clamped to $[4, 16]$ (both additionally bounded by $n_{\text{prompts}} - 1$).

In the DIVE framework, the evolution budget is adaptively allocated across heterogeneous operators using the Upper Confidence Bound (UCB) score:

$$UCB_a(t) = \hat{\mu}_a(t) + \beta \sqrt{\frac{\log t}{N_a(t)}}$$

where:
- $N_a(t)$ is the number of times operator $a$ has been selected before step $t$;
- $\hat{\mu}_a(t) = \frac{1}{N_a(t)} \sum_{\tau < t: a_\tau = a} R_\tau$ is the empirical mean parent-relative reward for operator $a$;
- $\beta$ is a coefficient balancing exploration of less frequently evaluated operators with exploitation of high-performing operators;
- Operators are initialized by being applied at least once, after which the operator maximizing $UCB_a(t)$ is selected: $a_t = \arg\max_{a \in \mathcal{A}} UCB_a(t)$.

The parent-relative reward $R_\tau$ at step $\tau$ is calculated as the improvement of the proposed candidate skill $s'_\tau$ over its best-performing parent in parent set $P_\tau$:

$$R_\tau \triangleq U(s'_\tau) - \max_{s \in P_\tau} U(s)$$

where $U(s)$ denotes the average correctness of skills.

