$$UCB_a(t) = \hat{\mu}_a(t) + \beta \sqrt{\frac{\log t}{N_a(t)}}$$

where:
- $N_a(t)$ is the number of times operator $a$ has been selected prior to step $t$;
- $\hat{\mu}_a(t) = \frac{1}{N_a(t)} \sum_{\tau < t: a_\tau = a} R_\tau$ is the empirical mean parent-relative reward for operator $a$;
- $\beta$ is the exploration parameter balancing exploitation of successful operators with exploration of less frequently evaluated ones;
- Operators are initially evaluated at least once, after which $a_t = \arg\max_{a \in \mathcal{A}} UCB_a(t)$ is selected.

The parent-relative reward $R_\tau$ at step $\tau$ is calculated as the improvement of the proposed offspring skill $s'_\tau$ relative to the highest-utility parent in $P_\tau$:

$$R_\tau \triangleq U(s'_\tau) - \max_{s \in P_\tau} U(s)$$

where $U(s)$ denotes the average correctness of skills.
